---
/**
 * The config reference, rendered from the release's `docs.json`.
 *
 * bushel derives that file from two places at once — keys and defaults from
 * `Config::default()`, flags and help text from clap's `Args` — and fails its
 * own build when the two disagree, which is what lets this page print a table
 * nobody maintains. The TOML block below is generated from the same options,
 * so it cannot list a key the table does not.
 *
 * Decided in https://github.com/frankieramirez/bushel/issues/47
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { bushelDocs } from "../../lib/bushelDocs";

const { version, config } = await bushelDocs();

/** TOML for the defaults, in the order bushel declares its flags. */
const example = config.options
  .map((option) => `${option.key} = ${JSON.stringify(option.default)}`)
  .join("\n");

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "options", text: "Options" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "flags-and-the-file", text: "Flags and the file" },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Config"
  description="bushel's config file options, their defaults, and the flags that set the same things."
  lede="Three switches, a file that holds them, and the flags that set the same things."
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    bushel reads <code>{config.path}</code>, which holds exactly what the flags
    can also set. The file is optional and every key below is optional inside it.
    This page is generated from bushel v{version}, so the defaults are the ones
    the binary actually starts with.
  </p>

  <p>Writing out the defaults gives you a file that changes nothing:</p>

  <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>

  <div class="docs-scroll">
    <table class="docs-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Flag</th>
          <th>Default</th>
          <th>Does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          config.options.map((option) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{option.key}</code>
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>{option.flag}</code>
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>{JSON.stringify(option.default)}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{option.desc}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="flags-and-the-file">Flags and the file</h2>

  <p>
    A flag can only switch something on. bushel ORs each flag with the file's
    value, so <code>reduced_motion = true</code> in the file stays on whatever
    you pass on the command line, and turning it back off means editing the file.
  </p>

  <p>
    A key bushel does not recognise is ignored in silence, so a misspelling like
    <code>reduce_motion</code> produces no warning and no effect. A value of the
    wrong type is louder: bushel prints one line naming the file and falls back
    to defaults for all of it.
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#settings-that-do-nothing">Troubleshooting</a>
    has both.
  </p>
</Docs>
